course: english
lesson: 31
title: Tense consistency and verb forms
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Tense is the time a verb points to: "I walked" is past, "I walk" is present, and "I will walk" talks about the future.
explain: A story is like a film shot from one seat in the cinema: if you write "I went to the park and I see a dog", the camera jumps from yesterday to now and the reader wobbles, so you keep one tense unless the time really changes.
explain: Each tense has helpers: "I am running" (am + -ing) is happening right now, "I was running" was going on in the past, and "I have eaten" (have + eaten) is past but still matters now, because you are full.
explain: Time words are clues: "yesterday" needs the past, "tomorrow" the future, so "Yesterday I will go to the zoo" is broken, like a clock running backwards.
explain: Homes and towns have their own verb habits: a Lancashire gran may say "I were going to't shop" ("to't" is "to the"), and that is real dialect with its own rules; in school writing the standard form is "I was going to the shop".
fact: Tense shows when something happens: past, present or future.
fact: The simple present ("I run") is used for habits and things that are generally true; the present progressive ("I am running") is for something happening right now.
fact: The progressive forms use a form of "be" plus a verb ending in -ing: "I am playing" (present), "I was playing" (past).
fact: The present perfect uses "have" or "has" plus a past form like eaten, gone or seen: "I have eaten" links a past action to now.
fact: The simple past ("I ate") tells about a finished action, often with a time: "I ate at six."
fact: "Had" plus a past form, like "had started", shows something that happened before another past event: "When we got there, the film had started" means the film started first.
fact: In a piece of writing, keep the same tense unless the time really changes; "I was tired, so I'm going to bed" changes on purpose, because the bed is now.
fact: Time words such as yesterday, now and tomorrow must agree with the tense of the verb.
fact: Modal verbs (might, could, should, must, will) show how sure, able or obliged someone is; in school grammar they are helping verbs, not tenses.
fact: In some Northern English dialects "were" is used with every person ("I were", "she were"); Standard English uses "I was" and "she was".
fact: "I been knowing that", from some American dialects including African American English, means "I have known that for a long time"; in standard writing it is "I've known that".
fact: "I seen it" is common in speech in many places; the standard forms are "I saw it" or "I have seen it".
fact: In London slang "bare" means very or lots of, as in "bare tired".
fact: Reading your work aloud is the quickest way to hear an accidental tense slip.
exercise: Ethan writes "I went to the park and I see a dog." What is wrong? | It jumps from past to present by accident | "Went" should be "goed" because it is past | It needs a future verb such as "will see" | "Park" should have a capital letter here
exercise: Fix Ethan's sentence. | I went to the park and I saw a dog. | I go to the park and I saw a dog. | I went to the park and I seen a dog. | I will go to the park and I saw a dog.
exercise: Which sentence is about something happening right now? | I am running to the gate. | I run to school every day. | I ran to the gate on Monday. | I have run there many times before.
exercise: "I run to school every day." What does this simple present show? | A habit | A plan for next week | Something that happened once yesterday | An action happening at this very second
exercise: Mum asks "Hungry?" and Zak says "No, I have eaten." Why is "have eaten" a good choice? | The eating is past but matters now: he is full | It shows he will eat later this evening | It is the only correct past form of eat | It shows he is eating at this moment
exercise: Max writes "Yesterday I will go to the zoo." Which word clashes with "yesterday"? | will go | to the zoo | I | go to
exercise: Year 6 test: change "I play football" to the past progressive. | I was playing football. | I played football. | I am playing football. | I have played football.
exercise: Kayla from London says "I was bare tired so I'm going to bed." ("Bare" means very.) Is the tense change a mistake? | No, the time really changes: tired before, bed now | Yes, a sentence must never change tense at all | Yes, "bare" can't be used with any past verb | No, because tense rules don't count in London
exercise: Gran in Lancashire says "I were going to't shop." What is the Standard English? | I was going to the shop. | I were going to the shop. | I was go to the shop. | I am going to't shop.
exercise: Aiden in Georgia says "I been knowing that." What does he mean, and how would standard writing say it? | He has known it a long time: "I've known that" | He is learning it now: "I am knowing that" | He forgot it: "I had known that" | He will find out: "I will know that"
exercise: A Year 5 story keeps switching between past and present. What is the teacher's advice? | Pick one tense and stick to it | Use both tenses in every single sentence | Change the tense in each new paragraph | Write the whole story in the future
exercise: Leo asks "Are might, could and should tenses?" What is the answer? | No, they are modal verbs that show how sure or how needed | Yes, they are the three future tenses of English | Yes, might is the past, could is the present and should is the future | No, they are nouns because they name feelings
exercise: Which is the past progressive? | We were singing. | We sing. | We sang. | We have sung.
exercise: Josh texts "i seen him at the chippy lol". What would Josh write in a school story? | I saw him at the chip shop. | I seen him at the chip shop. | I have saw him at the chip shop. | I sawed him at the chip shop.
exercise: Which sentence has the time word and the tense agreeing? | Tomorrow we will visit Nan. | Tomorrow we visited Nan. | Last week we will visit Nan. | Yesterday we are visiting Nan.
exercise: Why is reading your work aloud useful for tenses? | Your ear catches slips your eyes skip | It makes the writing longer and better | Teachers give marks for reading aloud | It changes every verb into the past
test: Your mate goes "tense is just for maths, innit, like tens and units." What is true? | Tense means the time a verb points to | Tense is the number of words in a sentence | Tense is how worried the writer feels | Tense means how loud you read a word
test: ★★★ "When we got to the cinema, the film had started." Which happened first? | The film starting | Getting to the cinema | Both at the same moment | Nothing has happened yet
test: Freya writes a whole story in the present: "I open the door. A fox stares at me." Is that allowed? | Yes, a story can be in the present if it stays there | No, every story must be in the past tense | No, the present is only for diaries and texts | Yes, but only if a fox is in it
test: In a cartoon, a robot says "Yesterday I will be fixing the car." What would a grammar teacher say? | Yesterday needs a past verb: "Yesterday I was fixing the car" | Robots may use any tense they like in any sentence they say | It is correct because it uses -ing | Change "yesterday" to "next year ago"
test: Older cousin, dead dry: "I have ate my tea, now shush." What is the standard form? | I have eaten my tea. | I have ated my tea. | I has eaten my tea. | I have eat my tea.
test: ★★★ Which sentence has an accidental tense slip? | The dog barked, so we run inside and hid. | The dog barks every night, so we keep the window shut. | I was cold yesterday, but I'm fine today. | We have lived here since I was two.
test: A lad from Wigan says "Our kid were late again, the muppet." ("Our kid" means my brother or sister.) Which is the standard? | My brother was late again. | My brother were late again. | My brother is late again yesterday. | My brother be late again.
test: What would happen if a story jumped tense in every sentence? | The reader would lose track of when things happen | The story would become far more exciting and much clearer to follow | Nothing, because readers ignore verbs | The story would turn into a poem
test: Bella says "'I have seen it' and 'I saw it' mean exactly the same, always." Which is closest to the truth? | Both are past, but "have seen" links it to now | "I saw it" is wrong in Standard English | "I have seen it" is the future | Only "I seen it" is correct
test: Nan, chuffed ("chuffed" means pleased): "You've grown since Christmas!" Which tense is "have grown"? | Present perfect | Simple future | Past progressive | Present progressive
test: ★★★ Fill in for the right tense: "While I ___ my homework, the lights went out." | was doing | am doing | will do | have done
exam: Identify the sentence in which the tense is consistent throughout. | I opened the box and found a key. | I opened the box and find a key. | I open the box and found a key. | I will open the box and found a key.
exam: Select the form designated the past progressive of the verb "to play" with the subject "I". | I was playing | I played | I am playing | I have played
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the words "might", "could" and "should"? | They are modal verbs expressing degrees of certainty or obligation | They are tense markers denoting the future in English | They are conjunctions joining two clauses | They are adverbs modifying nouns
exam: It is given that a speaker says "I were going to the shop". Ascertain the corresponding Standard English form. | I was going to the shop | I were went to the shop | I am going to the shop yesterday | I be going to the shop
